`join(left_on=..., right_on=...)` accepts expressions too.

### Naming: where output names come from {#sec-expr-naming}

dplyr names an unnamed output after its code (`mean(x)`). Polars uses these
rules instead:

1. The output name is the name of the **leftmost input** of the expression:
   `pl.col("a") / pl.col("b")` → `a`, and `pl.col("a").mean()` → `a`.
2. An expression that starts without a column gets a fixed name:
   `pl.lit(...)` → `literal` and `pl.len()` → `len`.
3. `pl.when(...).then(...)` takes its name from the first `then()`.
4. Override the name with a keyword argument (`name=expr`) or `.alias("name")`.
   Bulk renames use `.name.suffix()` and similar (see
   [Selectors](selectors.qmd)).
5. Names must be unique within one context.

::: {#5a1220d8 .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
penguins_df.select(
    pl.col("bill_length_mm") / pl.col("bill_depth_mm"),   # → bill_length_mm (!)
    pl.lit(1) + pl.col("year"),                            # → literal
    pl.len(),                                              # → len, broadcast
).head(2)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=16}
```{=html}
<div><style>
.dataframe > thead > tr,
.dataframe > tbody > tr {
  text-align: right;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
</style>
<small>shape: (2, 3)</small><table border="1" class="dataframe"><thead><tr><th>bill_length_mm</th><th>literal</th><th>len</th></tr><tr><td>f64</td><td>i64</td><td>u32</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2.090909</td><td>2008</td><td>344</td></tr><tr><td>2.270115</td><td>2008</td><td>344</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
```
:::
:::


Rule 1 plus rule 5 means a summary that works in dplyr fails in Polars:

::: {#af74f549 .cell execution_count=17}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# summarise(mean(body_mass_g), max(body_mass_g)) works in dplyr
penguins_df.select(pl.col("body_mass_g").mean(), pl.col("body_mass_g").max())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-error}

::: {.ansi-escaped-output}
```{=html}
<pre><span class="ansi-red-fg">DuplicateError</span><span class="ansi-red-fg">:</span> projections contained duplicate output name 'body_mass_g'. It's possible that multiple expressions are returning the same default column name. If this is the case, try renaming the columns with `.alias("new_name")` to avoid duplicate column names.
</pre>
```
:::

:::
:::


Name them:

::: {#ba5b9c17 .cell execution_count=18}
``` {.python .cell-code}
penguins_df.select(
    mass_mean=pl.col("body_mass_g").mean(),
    mass_max=pl.col("body_mass_g").max(),
)
```

